WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.440
Thanks to CuriosityStream for supporting this
episode!

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:09.160
Go to CuriosityStream.com/microcosmos to start
streaming thousands of documentaries and for

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:32.059
a special offer that helps support our show.

00:00:32.059 --> 00:00:35.600
Our world is made up of invisible highways.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:41.930
They intersect and divert and congest, taking
organisms on short trips and long voyages

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.550
alike in a flurry of activity.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:50.159
Now, we may not be able to see all of those
highways laid out, the way commuters might

00:00:50.159 --> 00:00:53.019
see long stretches of concrete stacked on
each other.

00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:57.999
But we can start to see their outlines in
the lives that are traced upon those roads.

00:00:57.999 --> 00:01:00.479
The microcosmos is no exception.

00:01:00.479 --> 00:01:06.009
In fact, it is where all those journeys begin.

00:01:06.009 --> 00:01:11.020
When Antonie van Leeuwenhoek made his first
observations of microbes, he was captivated

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:16.840
by those primitive paths, even though he didn’t
know what he was observing.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.600
He didn’t even know what creatures he was
observing.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:25.531
He wrote, “…the motion of most of these
animalcules in the water was so swift, and

00:01:25.531 --> 00:01:32.740
so various upwards, downwards and round about
that ’twas wonderful to see.”

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:38.079
Leeuwenhoek hired a draughtsman to help draw
what he observed.

00:01:38.079 --> 00:01:42.509
And sharing his employer’s delight, the
draughtsman reportedly exclaimed, “Oh, that

00:01:42.509 --> 00:01:45.920
one could ever depict so wonderful a motion!”

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:49.759
He probably did it in Dutch but you get the
idea.

00:01:49.759 --> 00:01:54.740
The turn of the 18th century was not a particularly
great time for video.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:02:02.700
But fortunately, times have changed, and now
we have ways to depict so wonderful a motion.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:09.149
And that means we can share things like this,
a band of bacteria—or really more like a

00:02:09.149 --> 00:02:16.450
cylinder of thousands of bacteria—that have
gathered for some unseeable reason that must

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:18.090
be important to them.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:22.360
We think—though we can’t quite be sure—that
the culprit is oxygen.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:26.849
James, our master of microscopes, kept the
slides in a humidity chamber that was low

00:02:26.849 --> 00:02:30.890
in oxygen—an environment that was well-suited
for these bacteria.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:36.069
But when he took the slide out to check it
under the microscope, oxygen began to seep

00:02:36.069 --> 00:02:37.840
in from the sides.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.290
And while many organisms love oxygen, these
bacteria do not.

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:47.470
And with their habitat being slowly encroached
upon by the gas, the bacteria gathered in

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:51.770
the middle of the slide where the oxygen is
lower.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:55.510
If you take a quick glance, the band of bacteria
seems pretty orderly.

00:02:55.510 --> 00:03:02.159
But it just takes a few more seconds to see
that it is made up of chaos, bacteria that

00:03:02.159 --> 00:03:06.849
are swimming and wiggling around in what seems
like a completely undirected fashion.

00:03:06.849 --> 00:03:10.260
Except, of course, it is directed.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:19.370
They’re navigating inside an invisible road
shaped by oxygen.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:26.890
In 1882, the scientist Theodor Wilhelm Engelmann
used a microscope designed by Carl Zeiss himself

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:29.450
to watch algae as they photosynthesized.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:33.719
But it wasn’t just the algae he was interested
in.

00:03:33.719 --> 00:03:41.030
Engelmann also watched as oxygen-loving bacteria
congregated to the areas where algae lay,

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:45.560
seeking out the oxygen byproduct of their
neighbor’s photosynthetic activity.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:53.719
Engelmann’s work provided descriptions of
bacteria changing their behavior around invisible chemicals.

00:03:53.719 --> 00:03:59.359
But it would take a contemporary of his, and
then several decades of waiting, for scientists

00:03:59.359 --> 00:04:03.300
to figure out the mechanisms underlying those
changes.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:09.040
His contemporary was named Wilhelm Pfeffer,
and he designed an experiment using a thin

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:16.130
capillary tube filled with chemical solutions
to study how bacteria gathered first to the

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:18.750
tube, and then inside of it.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:24.250
After Pfeffer the field becomes quieter until
the middle of the 20th century, when a scientist

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:30.240
named Julius Adler began searching for his
next research project, the one he would pursue

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:35.950
after studying butterflies in college and
rat liver mitochondria in grad school.

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:42.080
It was 1957, and he just happened to be going
through a library filled with old scientific

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:45.790
journals, where he found Pfeffer’s publications.

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:52.070
What he read would end up inspiring decades
of his work, as well as the work of his students,

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:58.370
and colleagues to decipher the chemical and
biological language that bacteria were using

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:01.690
to navigate the world around them.

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:05.880
He established, for instance, that bacteria
are able to detect molecules around them without

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:15.220
actually consuming those molecules, using
receptors at their surface to process and respond.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:19.860
One student, Mel DePamphilis, used an electron
microscope to capture images of the bacteria’s

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:21.280
motor, called the flagella.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:27.050
Later, he would say, “I was so excited I
could not take photos but kept thinking, ‘My

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:33.370
God, I’m the first person to see this.’”

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:37.680
A tiny peek behind the curtain, there’s
a note on the script here, and James, our

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:41.680
Master of Microscopes has made a comment,
and he says, “Aha!

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:44.650
This happens to me all the time!”

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:49.750
What a joyous feeling that must be, but also
look, all of us together are seeing that thing

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:55.940
that Mel DePamphilis first saw all those decades
ago and it is no less remarkable for having

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:57.830
been seen by others.

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:04.550
Anyway, over time, what became apparent is
that the flagella doesn’t just move bacteria,

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:07.960
because swimming in the microcosmos is kind
of difficult when you’re small.

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:10.420
The water is thick, like honey.

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:16.330
But what the bacteria’s combination of flagella
and receptors do is let the organism move

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:21.620
randomly…but also purposefully.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:26.180
The systems can vary among different bacteria—they
do, after all, have different needs from their

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:30.550
environments but so much of what we know comes
from studies of E. coli.

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:35.790
And in E. coli, the movement of the flagella
dictates whether the organism will tumble

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:38.210
around or propel forward.

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:41.710
Now, why would an organism want to tumble?

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:44.100
What does randomly turning about do?

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:50.070
Well, for you and I, tumbling is probably,
at best, not useful, at worst, disorienting,

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:55.690
but for bacteria, it’s a way to get reoriented,
allowing them to point in different directions

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:58.500
until they find the right way forward.

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:04.570
And to determine what the right way is, they
measure gradients, looking for changes in

00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:10.660
the amount of those chemicals they either
want to get closer to or further away from.

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:15.100
When those changes point to something good,
the bacteria propels onward.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:17.950
And when they point to something bad, it tumbles.

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:25.630
They can do this incredibly fast, capable
of speeds that are several hundred body lengths per second.

00:07:25.630 --> 00:07:31.740
It’s like sprinting home in the dark, guided
only by the familiar changes in the streets

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:37.590
around you but it is enough to have helped
bacteria survive this world and its many,

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:40.370
many challenges for billions of years.

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:46.460
Of course, it also means that other organisms
have inherited those mechanisms too, like

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:51.660
these ciliates drawn as they are to their
bacterial prey through an invisible connection,

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:55.750
whether or not they knew that’s what drew
them there in the first place.

00:07:55.750 --> 00:08:02.160
Because even when the roads are unseen, and
even when we may not know where we’re going,

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:10.690
we can surely figure out what to do when we
get there.

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:16.060
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

